  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nRain slowly ending over eastern WI this afternoon. A few spots\nhave reached 0.50 inch of rain, but most locations have stayed\nwell under 0.25 inch. No rain over north-central and far northeast\nWI, where it is desperately needed. Chances of rain moving \nforward will be few and far between. Shortwave trough and \nassociated sfc low zips across the upper Great Lakes Saturday \nnight. An isolated light shower could form as early as Saturday \nafternoon, but greatest chances (30-50%) will occur Saturday \nnight. Light QPF expected with only 10-15% chance of seeing \nanything more than 0.25 inch. Next chance of rain, showing up more\nin the deterministic models versus ensembles does not arrive \nuntil next Wednesday. High pressure will be the primary weather \nfeature around through that time. \n\nTemperatures/Frost: Main impact in the weather will be cooler\ntemps and frost/freeze potential. Risk starts up as early as\ntonight. Better chances, by far, set up for Friday night into\nearly Saturday, then again Sunday night into early Monday. NBM\nprobabilities of seeing temps less than 33F surge to 50-90% mainly\nfrom Rhinelander to Marinette northward. When raising the temp \nthreshold up to lower than 36, the risk for frost expands into \ncentral WI (30-60%) Sunday night into early Monday. Possible that\nfrost risk could even expand toward the Fox Valley this time as \nwell, and late Monday night into Tuesday morning as the high  \ncenter drifts overhead. Headlines will be needed as we have not\nstopped the Frost/Freeze program yet for the season.",
